","The coordination geometry of the iron (III) ion in iron (III) tetraphenylporphyrin complex is a distorted octahedral geometry. This is because the iron ion is coordinated to the four nitrogen atoms of the porphyrin ring, which are arranged in a square planar arrangement, and two additional axial ligands.

The coordination geometry of the iron (III) ion in iron (III) tetrapphyrin complex is influenced by several factors:

1. Steric repulsion: The bulky tetraphenyl group on the porphyrin ring causes significant steric repulsion, which distorts the octahedral geometry. This repulsion causes the axial ligands to be positioned in a position that minimizes repulsion with the tetraphenyl groups.

2. Electronic effects: The iron ion is in the +3 oxidation state, which means it has six electrons in its d-orbital. The coordination of the four nitrogen atoms of the porphyrin ring causes a change in the electronic structure of the iron ion, which can also influence the coordination geometry.

