<USER_REQUEST>
A solution has a hydronium ion concentration \([H_3O^+]\) of \(10^{-4}\) M. What is the nature of this solution (acidic, basic, or neutral)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The hydronium ion concentration \([H_3O^+]\) is given as \(10^{-4}\) M. To determine the nature of the solution, we compare \([H_3O^+]\) to the concentration of hydroxide ions \([OH^-]\). In a neutral solution, \([H_3O^+] = [OH^-] = 10^{-7}\) M. The relationship between \([H_3O^+]\) and \([OH^-]\) is given by the ion product of water (\(K_w\)):
\[
[H_3O^+][OH^-] = K_w = 10^{-14}
\]
Since \([H_3O^+] = 10^{-4}\) M, we can find \([OH^-]\):
\[
[OH^-] = \frac{10^{-14}}{10^{-4}} = 10^{-10} \text{ M}
\]
Since \([H_3O^+] > [OH^-]\) (i.e., \(10^{-4} > 10^{-10}\)), the solution is acidic. 

Thus, the correct answer is \(\boxed{\text{acidic}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

