In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the distribution of electron density:  
1. σ (sigma) molecular orbitals have electron density that is concentrated **directly above and below the internuclear bond** between the nuclei of the atoms.  
2. π (pi) molecular orbitals have electron density that is concentrated **above and below the internuclear bond**, but not directly above or below it.  

Thus, σ orbitals are symmetrical with respect to the intern of the bond, while π orbitals are not.  

\boxed{\text{The π orbital has electron density above and below the internuclear bond, but not directly above or below, while the σ orbital has electron density directly above and below the internuclear bond.}}",fluency,"# Task Description
