The third law of thermodynamics states that the entropy of a perfect crystal at absolute zero is zero. This law states that the entropy of a system approaches zero as the temperature approaches absolute zero. It does not state that the entropy of a system is zero at absolute zero, only that it approaches zero. Thus, the law implies that the entropy of imperfect crystals at absolute zero is greater than zero. This is because imperfect crystals have defects, such as vacancies, dislocations, and dislocations, which introduce entropy. These defects allow for more possible configurations of the system, which increases the entropy.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

