To pave a rectangular courtyard 50 m long and 16 1/2 m wide, 165 paving stones, each measuring 2 1/2 m * a certain width, are required. What is the width of each paving stone?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The area of the rectangular courtyard is:
\[
\text{Area} = \text{Length} \times \text{Width} = 50 \text{ m} \times 16.25 \text{ m} = 812.5 \text{ m}^2.
\]
Each paving stone is 2.25 m long. Let the width of each paving stone be \( x \) cm. The area of each paving stone is:
\[
\text{Area of paving stone} = 2.25 \text{ m} \times x \text{ m} = 2.25x \text{ m}^2.
\]
165 paving stones are required, so the total area of the paving stones is:
\[
165 \times 2.25x = 370.5x \text{ m}^2.
\]
The total area of the paving stones must be equal to the area of the rectangular courtyard:
\[
370.5x = 812.5.
\]
Solving for \( x \):
\[
x = \frac{812.5}{370.5} = 2.19 \text{ m}.
\]
Thus, the width of each paving stone is \(\boxed{2.19 \text{ m}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

