\[
n = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{1 + 4a^2}}{2}
\]
For \( n \) to be an integer, \( 1 + 4a^2 \) must be a perfect square, i.e., \( 1 + 4a^2 = k^2 \) for some integer \( k \). Rearranging, we get \( k^2 - 4a^2 = 1 \), which simplifies to \( (k - 2a)(k + 2a) = 1 \). Since \( k \) and \( a \) are integers, the only possible solutions are:
\[
k - 2a = 1 \quad \text{and} \quad k + 2a = 1
\]
