An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The forward gain \( A_v = 100 \). The feedback ratio \( m_v = 0.1 \). The gain \( A_v \) decreases by 6 dB due to ageing.  

1. **Calculate the new forward gain \( A_v' \):**  
   A 6 dB decrease in gain corresponds to a factor of \( 10^{-6/10} = 0.5 \).  
   Thus, the new forward gain is \( A_v' = 0.5 \times A_v = 0.5 \times 100 = 50 \).  

2. **Calculate the original closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \):**  
   The closed-loop gain is given by:  
   \[
   A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_v A_v} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1 \times 100} = \frac{100}{1 - 10} = \frac{100}{-9} \approx -11.11 \text{ dB}
   \]  
   (The negative sign indicates the feedback is inverting.)  

3. **Calculate the new closed-loop gain \( A_{vf}' \):**  
   Using the new forward gain \( A_v' = 50 \):  
   \[
   A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 - m_v A_v'} = \frac{50}{1 - 0.1 \times 50} = \frac{50}{1 - 5} = \frac{50}{-4} = -12.5 \text{ dB}
   \]  

4. **Calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain:**  
   \[
   \text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{-12.5 - (-11.11)}{-11.11} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{-1.39}{-11.11} \right) \times 100 \approx 12.5\%
   \]  

**Final Answer:**  
The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is approximately \(\boxed{12.5\%}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
