","The net chemical reaction for the discharging of a lead storage battery is:  
\[ \text{Zn} + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow \text{ZnSO}_4 + \text{Zn}^{2+} + 2\text{e}^- \]  
This reaction occurs at the cathode (negative electrode) and produces a solution of \(\text{Zn}^{2+}\) ions.  

The other half-reaction occurs at the anode (positive electrode) and is:  
\[ \text{Pb} + \2\text{H}^+ + \text{e}^- \rightarrow \text{Pb}^{2+} + \text{H}_2 \]  
This reaction occurs at the anode (positive electrode) and produces \(\text{Pb}^{2+}\) ions.  

The overall reaction is:  
