","The decay length (\(\lambda_d\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:  
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi \Delta n}
\]  
where:  
- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the evanescent wave.  
- \(\Delta n\) is the refractive difference between the material the evanescent wave is propagating into and the material it was originally in.  

For a GaAs core and Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As cladding, the evanescent wave is evanescent because the refractive index of the cladding is higher than the core.  

Given:  
- \(\lambda = 1 \, \mu\text{m} = 1000 \, \mu\text{m}\)  
- \(\Delta n = n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \approx 0.3\)  

Substituting these values into the formula:  
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{1000 \, \mu\text{m}}{2\pi \times 0.3} \approx 1696 \, \mu\text{m}
\]  
